From: ThorOS maintainers
Subject: [PATCH] netfilter: do not regress a flow deadline on a delayed refresh

A software packet can sample its deadline, lose the CPU, and later overwrite
a newer software or hardware-stats refresh. Unsigned subtraction also treats
an older deadline as a huge forward jump. Compare signed remaining lifetimes
against a fresh clock for each CAS attempt, using s64 for their difference:
a legal near-INT_MAX duration and an expired old deadline can differ by more
than half of the u32 cycle. Keep the >HZ debounce without a packet-path lock.
Hardware stats use the same monotonic update rule.

The force caller in act_ct requests a hardware state/reply-direction refresh.
Keep that request even when its sampled deadline no longer moves forward;
force is not permission to overwrite a newer timeout. Reducing the timeout
sysctl affects later extensions but does not shorten an already granted
deadline, matching the hardware-stats path's monotonic lifetime contract.

--- a/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table_core.c
+++ b/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table_core.c
@@ -380,13 +380,26 @@
 void flow_offload_refresh(struct nf_flowtable *flow_table,
 			  struct flow_offload *flow, bool force)
 {
-	u32 timeout;
+	u32 timeout, old, now;
+	s64 advance;
 
 	timeout = nf_flowtable_time_stamp + flow_offload_get_timeout(flow);
-	if (force || timeout - READ_ONCE(flow->timeout) > HZ)
-		WRITE_ONCE(flow->timeout, timeout);
-	else
-		return;
+	old = READ_ONCE(flow->timeout);
+	for (;;) {
+		/* A delayed packet must not lower a newer stats/packet deadline.
+		 * force still requests the act_ct hardware-state refresh below.
+		 */
+		now = nf_flowtable_time_stamp;
+		advance = (s64)(__s32)(timeout - now) - (__s32)(old - now);
+		if ((__s32)(timeout - now) <= 0 ||
+		    advance <= (force ? 0 : (__s32)HZ)) {
+			if (!force)
+				return;
+			break;
+		}
+		if (try_cmpxchg(&flow->timeout, &old, timeout))
+			break;
+	}
 
 	if (likely(!nf_flowtable_hw_offload(flow_table)) ||
 	    test_bit(NF_FLOW_CLOSING, &flow->flags))
